Pantheon · Math to Myth

A fieldbook for making stories from numbers, music, and the things characters can’t let go of.

Give a number a role, give an operation a consequence, and let the reader work out what the characters can do next. A story can carry the same pattern as an equation, a constellation, or a score. Each form can reveal something the others leave implicit.

Keep this file as a writing reference or paste the recipe at the end into a chat. A finished story can live in its own Markdown file, with the mapping table appended if you want to preserve how it was made. Start with an equation, a short musical phrase, or a scene whose ending you haven’t solved.

Cast the structure

1. Choose the problem. What does somebody want, and what makes it hard to get? If the seed is an equation, solve it first. If the seed is a scene, pick a mathematical rule that changes somebody’s choice.
2. Assign roles. Give recurring values or symbols recognizable characters, objects, places, or cues. Write down what each one can do. A number might be a fixed crossing time, a debt, or a count of repetitions. Give characters desires of their own.
3. Stage the operations. Resolve dependencies in the right order. Each operation gets a beat in which somebody acts and a state changes. Independent operations can happen in parallel skits. Bring them together when one scene needs the result of the others.
4. Overlay the voices. Give two threads different motifs. You can distinguish them by rhythm, timbre, texture, or spatial position. Give every recurring cue a bearer and somebody who can act on it. In a coordination plot, identify who can hear whom, what each person is waiting for, and what happens when two cues arrive together.
5. Pay it off. Let the audience learn a rule early and use it to anticipate a later choice. The same cue can change meaning when its timing, speaker, or recipient changes. Leave room for a laugh, surprise, or reversal that follows from the characters’ choices.
6. Check the two versions. Walk the calculation forward. Then read the prose on its own. The math should describe what actually happened, and the scene should make sense on a cold read.

Operations as scenes

|Symbol or structure|Dramatic move                                          |Rule to preserve                                                                                                               |
|-------------------|-------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|`a + b`            |Join two routes or pools of resources                  |The second displacement starts where the first ends.                                                                           |
|`a − b`            |Retrace another traveler’s route from your own position|Reverse the second displacement, then add it.                                                                                  |
|`a × b`            |One traveler gives another a bearing and scale         |For complex numbers, multiply distances from the origin and add angles. Zero sends the result to the origin.                   |
|`a ÷ b`            |Remove the turn and scale introduced by a traveler     |Subtract the divisor’s angle and divide by its distance. The divisor must be nonzero.                                          |
|`aⁿ`               |Rehearse the same transformation                       |For positive integer powers, apply the same multiplier repeatedly starting from 1.                                             |
|Parentheses        |Give a pair a private scene before they rejoin the cast|That result becomes an input to the enclosing operation.                                                                       |
|Conjugation        |Let two people hold reflections of the same event      |Keep the real coordinate and reverse the imaginary one.                                                                        |
|`i`                |A character who gives directions across the grain      |Multiplying by `i` maps `(x, y)` to `(−y, x)`. Four quarter-turns return a point to its starting position.                     |
|A dependency graph |Braid separate plots around shared cues                |A scene can start once all of its required inputs are present. A circular dependency needs a starting signal or a changed rule.|

The complex coordinate can be tied in knots in the telling. Use the constellation to track where each strand is, the score to track when it moves, and the plot to track who can set it moving. If two scenes end at the same coordinate, their paths can still carry different histories.

Melomancy and the forms of mania

Within this fieldbook, melomancy means reading a musical pattern as instructions for a story. Start with a phrase you can hum, tap, or write down. Decide which parts carry information before writing the scenes.

|What you hear                                   |What it can do in a plot                                              |
|------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------|
|A motif returns with one note changed           |Someone repeats an old choice with a different consequence.           |
|Two independent melodies arrive at the same beat|Two characters reach a rendezvous by separate routes.                 |
|One voice stops while the other keeps going     |A character loses a cue and has to take direction from a new source.  |
|A tune passes from a soloist to the room        |Responsibility passes from a leader to the ensemble.                  |
|A phrase ends a beat early                      |Somebody acts before the expected cue. Decide who can recover and how.|

Use mania here in the literary sense of an absorbing fixation. Give it a repeatable move and a price. These are prompts for fictional characters.

|Fixation  |What the character keeps doing                                  |What can change at the climax                                                   |
|----------|----------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|Counting  |Counts everything that enters, leaves, or goes missing          |Notices that one person has been counted twice and someone else has had no turn.|
|Collecting|Saves one token from every encounter                            |Gives away the only token that could prove where they’ve been.                  |
|Naming    |Gives everything a name, including anonymous noises             |Asks a stranger what name they chose for themself.                              |
|Returning |Revisits the same door or repeats the same operation            |Brings someone else along who can recognize the detail they keep missing.       |
|Tuning    |Changes pitch, rhythm, or temperature until the room feels right|Lets somebody else choose what “right” sounds like.                             |

You can also write a story around bibliomancy as a fictional reading of a line from a book, or oneiromancy as a fictional reading of a dream. Each can supply a clue or a character’s mistaken interpretation. The story’s world decides whether the reading has power.

Teach them to see

Give one motif several carriers. A gold thread can also be a smooth cord and a held note. An indigo thread can also be a knotted cord and two taps. A character can recognize the crossing by touch or sound. If the story uses Braille to name a color, spell out the color on an actual label in the imagined space. Use cord texture or raised paths to represent the map itself. The important thing is that the choice a character makes remains legible through the channel they have.

Worked story · The Unknot

Rule. ((1+i) × i) ÷ i = 1+i. The first turn sends (1, 1) to (−1, 1). The second returns it to (1, 1).

Cast. The Lantern Keeper is the traveler at 1+i. Quarter-Turn is the guide who lends their orientation. The Counting Woman knows the pattern the whole room will use.

Score. One tap introduces the Keeper. Two brisk taps mark the turn. When the lights fail, the same two taps followed by a held beat cue the return. Inside the story, the held beat is how the audience distinguishes the way home from the way out.

The lantern rehearsal

The Lantern Keeper had promised to teach the children how to find their way around the hall in the dark. He had booked a thunder machine to make it convincing.

Quarter-Turn laid a raised map on the table. Lark ran her thumb along the smooth cord. Where it crossed the knotted one, she found the starting peg.

“How will I know which way?” she asked.

“Two taps to send him out,” Quarter-Turn said. “Two taps and hold the beat to bring him home.”

Quarter-Turn tapped twice. The Keeper took the left-hand stair and raised his lantern on the west balcony. The Counting Woman ticked off the two taps on her fingers.

Then the power cut and the lantern’s bulb went out. Somewhere downstairs the thunder machine clicked uselessly. The Keeper called, “Which way back?”

The Counting Woman tapped twice on the back of a chair. The boy beside her started to tap a third time. She caught his wrist and waited a full beat. Then Lark tapped the pattern against the map table. Chairs all the way to the foot of the stairs took it up.

The Keeper made the opposite turn and came down beside Lark. She felt him set the cold lantern back in its ring on the table.

“Did you plan the bit where the lights went out?” she asked.

“I paid extra for the part where they stayed on.”

The room laughed. The Counting Woman held up two fingers out of habit. This time everybody waited to see what Lark would do.

She tapped once.

Read the construction

|Beat                     |Formal move                                |Human move                                          |
|-------------------------|-------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------|
|Find the peg             |Establish `(1, 1)`                         |Lark finds a known starting point by touch.         |
|Cross to the west balcony|Multiply by `i`, landing at `(−1, 1)`      |Quarter-Turn cues the departure.                    |
|Lose the electric cue    |Disrupt the signal while the position holds|The audience passes along the return instruction.   |
|Recover the lantern ring |Divide by `i`, returning to `(1, 1)`       |The Keeper arrives where Lark can verify it herself.|
|Tap once                 |Begin a new cue after the equation resolves|Lark gets to set the next move.                     |

